5.  ATTACK: is 1+2sqrt3 really the LEAST a at which 15 pieces are necessary?
==============================================================================
  [ok ] 15 lattice points listed
  [ok ] all 15 lie in the closed T_4
  [ok ] pairwise squared distances all >= 1 (min is exactly 1) :: (1+0r3)
  [ok ] 4 < 1 + 2 sqrt3 :: 4 < 4.464102
  => a piece of diameter <1 holds at most one of these 15 points, so EVERY
     covering of T_4 by diameter-<1 pieces uses >= 15 pieces:  N(4) >= 15.
     N is non-decreasing in a, so 15 pieces are necessary for all a >= 4,
     i.e. already 0.4641 BELOW 1+2sqrt3.  Theorem N at a = 4 forces only 10 pieces.
  [ok ] Theorem N is off by 5 pieces at a = 4 (forces 10, truth is >= 15)
  cross-check against the cited table: s(15) = 8+2sqrt3 (Oler, triangular
     number, `cited`) means d(15) = 8 at separation 2, i.e. a_15 = 4 at
     separation 1 -- so 4 is exactly the least side holding 15 separated points.

